evidence.statement:
# 2. Initial Evidence Trigger — Jefferies

ServiceNow's customer story describes Jefferies' post-trade settlement operations.

Jefferies processes hundreds of thousands of daily trades. Most settlements proceed without manual intervention, but settlement breaks require operational handling before deadlines.

The legacy process involved operations staff:

- pulling reports from systems of record;
- manually filtering exceptions;
- manually classifying exceptions;
- manually prioritizing exceptions;
- sorting large volumes of email;
- assigning work;
- correlating communications with exceptions using spreadsheets or documents.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/customers/jefferies.html

The associated ServiceNow case study provides the same operational description.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/content/dam/servicenow-assets/public/en-us/doc-type/resource-center/case-study/cs-jefferies.pdf

**Classification:** VERIFIED EXTERNAL EVIDENCE

# 3. What ServiceNow Already Solved

The Jefferies case states that ServiceNow created an overlay for exception workflows.

The solution:

- generated exception workflows directly from system-of-record data;
- converted emails into work items;
- connected communications to related workflows;
- provided management visibility;
- created an audit trail;
- reduced manual exception identification and classification.

The case reports that a CSDR-violation proof of concept reduced resolution time from approximately 30 minutes to a few minutes.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/customers/jefferies.html

**Classification:** DIRECT EVIDENCE

Therefore OPP-002 cannot be:

> “ServiceNow needs AI to classify and route exceptions.”

That capability is already substantially addressed.

