## 2.1 Help Optimize Team Productivity

ServiceNow's current platform includes an agentic workflow called **Help optimize team productivity**.

The documentation states that it:

- analyzes historical performance of team members;
- calculates workloads relative to each team member's typical capacity;
- improves assignments;
- balances workloads;
- provides proactive team-management insights.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/intelligent-experiences/help-optimize-productivity.html

**Classification: DIRECT EVIDENCE**

This is directly relevant to the OPP-002 hypothesis.

# 6. ServiceNow Also Supports Dynamic Reallocation

ServiceNow Dynamic Scheduling can:

- prioritize tasks;
- reassign previously assigned tasks;
- adapt to changing conditions;
- account for agent skills;
- account for travel time;
- reassign work when agents become unavailable;
- respond to newly arising high-priority tasks.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/Configure-dynamic-scheduling.html

The current Dynamic Scheduling documentation further states that tasks can be reassigned when new high-priority work arises and that capacity reservations can allocate capacity across different work attributes.

Source:

https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/dynamic-scheduling.html

**Classification: DIRECT EVIDENCE**

This is a major falsification result.

# 8. Analytical / Hypothetical Evidence

Because public customer evidence does not expose internal post-implementation decision logs, the investigation also examined the closest defensible analytical evidence.

The general operations-research literature treats the following as established classes of decision problems:

- scheduling under limited resources;
- prioritization under deadlines;
- resource-constrained scheduling;
- multi-objective scheduling;
- dispatching under changing conditions;
- optimization under competing constraints.

These analytical models establish that portfolio-level allocation can be a legitimate mathematical decision problem.

However, they do **not** establish:

- that a ServiceNow customer has this exact problem;
- that ServiceNow cannot solve it;
- that a specialized external engine would outperform ServiceNow;
- that customers would pay for such a system.

**Classification: VERIFIED ANALYTICAL EVIDENCE**

Analytical evidence therefore proves the **coherence of the problem class**, not the commercial gap.

